List notes, newest first (maps to note.list, D241)

Usage: tasqx notes [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --json               Print the raw JSON API result instead of the human table
      --links              Only notes holding an http:// or https:// link
      --archived           Archived notes instead of open ones
      --theme <THEME>      Theme name (built-in nord|gruvbox|dracula|solarized|mono, or a user file). Overrides $TASQX_THEME and the config file (DESIGN.md §8)
      --all                Open and archived notes
      --socket <SOCKET>    Local socket / named-pipe address of a tasqx daemon. Overrides $TASQX_SOCK. When a daemon is reachable, one-shot commands route through it (single writer); otherwise they run in-process. A set $TASQX_DB naming a different store than that daemon's is refused. Also selects the address for `daemon` and `watch`
      --no-daemon          Never route through a daemon; always run one-shot in-process (the pre-daemon behaviour). Escape hatch for scripts
      --search <TEXT>      Only notes whose text contains this, ignoring case
      --project <PROJECT>  Only notes filed under this project
  -h, --help               Print help
  -V, --version            Print version

EXAMPLES
  tasqx notes
  tasqx notes --links
  tasqx notes --search exporter --all    # a substring, ignoring case, in open and archived notes

NOTE  One line per note: its number, its first line, a link mark (`↗`, `L` without Unicode) when it holds an http:// or https:// link, its project and the day it was added. A note that is only a link is never cut, so it stays copyable; its row runs past the terminal instead.
      Open notes by default; --archived shows the archived ones (promoted notes among them), --all both. --search is a plain substring, not a ranked search.
      `tasqx note <id>` prints one note whole.
      On a terminal it opens a full-screen browser over the same notes instead, and the flags set where it opens; piped, or with --json, it prints the table. `j`/`k` move, `g`/`G` jump to the ends, `/` searches the text, `enter` reads a note whole (a link is shown whole, never opened), `esc` clears the search or leaves, `q` leaves. `a` archives or unarchives the note under the cursor, `t` and `m` promote it to a task or a memory doc, `x` removes it after a y/n, `l` keeps only the notes holding a link. Every write carries the revision the screen read, so a note changed elsewhere is a conflict on the screen, not a lost write. The dashboard's `N` opens the same browser.

See also: note · memory        Full manual: tasqx manual
